  "id": "Compound::DB00508 - Gene::1812",
  "verbalization": "Triflupromazine is likely to interact with Drd1 through a mechanism involving phenothiazines, as it resembles both Dimetotiazine and Propiomazine, which in turn bind to Drd1. This suggests a potential pathway where Triflupromazine, by resembling these compounds, may exert a similar effect on Drd1. The evidence is further supported by the direct interaction between Triflupromazine and Chrm2, which is involved in G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity, a process that is also engaged by Drd1. The shared ancestry of Triflupromazine with Phenothiazines and its role in antipsychotic activity points to a broader pharmacological context where Triflupromazine may influence Drd1 through a receptor-mediated mechanism. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible link between Triflupromazine and Drd1, mediated through receptor activity and neurotransmitter signaling.",
